  "id": "knee-osteoarthritis",
  "name": "Knee osteoarthritis",
  "kind": "FOCUSED",
  "category": "REHAB",
  "goal": "Less pain and easier walking, stairs and getting up from chairs, through regular strength and movement. Exercise is the first-line treatment for knee arthritis at any age.",
  "whoFor": "People with knee osteoarthritis (wear-and-tear arthritis): aching, stiffness after rest, and pain with stairs or walking.",
  "notFor": [
    "A hot, red, very swollen knee: see a doctor first"
  ],
  "estMinutes": 20,
  "trackedMetric": "Knee pain when walking or on stairs (0-10)",
  "painRules": "Pain up to 5 out of 10 during exercise is acceptable if it settles within 24 hours. If it doesn't, do a bit less next time rather than stopping.",
  "redFlags": [
    "The knee locks, gives way, or won't fully straighten",
    "Fast, large swelling after an injury",
    "A hot, red, swollen knee, or fever",
    "Calf pain with swelling and warmth (possible blood clot)",
    "Pain after a fall or twist that hasn't been checked",
    "Severe pain at night that's unrelated to activity"
  ],
